.SH "NAME"
.PP
xsigner \- hold TLS private keys so the proxies do not have to
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBxsigner\fR [\fB\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-validate\fR] [\fB\-print\-keys\fR] [\fB\-version\fR]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBxsigner\fR listens on a Unix socket and answers signature requests. A proxy sends a digest and gets a signature back, and the private key never crosses the socket.
.PP
What that buys is custody. A TLS private key in the proxy\(aqs memory is reachable by anything that can read that memory \[u2014] a core dump, a debugger, a read primitive in a bug. Moving the key into another process means there is nothing there to read: an attacker who owns the proxy can ask for signatures while they own it, which is bounded in time, and that is not the same as walking away with the key.
.PP
It is also where cgo belongs. A PKCS#11 module is a vendor C library and \fBxproxy\fR(8) and its siblings are built with \fBCGO_ENABLED=0\fR on purpose, so an HSM, a TPM or a smartcard is reached from here rather than from the process that terminates TLS for the estate. This build serves keys it can read as PEM \[u2014] from a file, the environment or a vault. The protocol is one JSON object each way over a Unix socket and is specified in \fBdocs/SIGNER.md\fR, so a helper for a device this build cannot reach can be written in whatever language that device has bindings for.
.PP
\fBThe socket\(aqs permissions are the authentication.\fR A digest says nothing about what it is a digest of, so anything that can connect to the socket can have anything signed by the keys this helper holds. There is no credential on the wire and there is deliberately no request that returns a key. The shipped arrangement is \fB/run/xsigner\fR at mode 2750 owned \fBxsigner:xsigner\-clients\fR with the socket inside it at 0660 \[u2014] the proxy daemons are members of that group, connecting to a Unix socket needs write permission on it, and the directory is what keeps everything else away. Widen neither.
.PP
Every key is resolved and parsed before the socket is bound, so a helper that could not answer for one of its keys is found out by an operator rather than by a handshake.
